490
00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:10.870
Zoe, immediately defensive, fires back, What is that supposed to mean?

491
00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:13.650
Olivia, smirking, says,

492
00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:14.530
Nothing.

493
00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:18.050
Just that some people pretend they're innocent when they're not.

494
00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:24.490
At this point, I didn't even know what was happening, but I definitely felt the tension.

495
00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:29.770
Then Jake, who had been drinking as much wine as Olivia, just blurts out,

496
00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.750
Wait, is this about Zoe and Damien?

497
00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:34.530
Who's Damien?

498
00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:35.410
Lucas writes.

499
00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:40.770
Now, Damien, who is also in the book club because he was good friends with Olivia,

500
00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:44.150
had been scrolling his phone like he wasn't a part of the conversation,

501
00:22:44.150 --> 00:22:46.430
and suddenly looks up, panicked.

502
00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:52.010
Zoe goes white, and Bethany, Damien's actual girlfriend at the time,

503
00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.550
just turns to him completely silent.

504
00:22:55.370 --> 00:22:56.470
Lucas says,

505
00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:57.930
Nobody is breathing.

506
00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.250
Zoe breaks first and says,

507
00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:02.050
Are you serious right now?

508
00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:03.550
Damien says,

509
00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:05.970
I don't—what are you guys talking about?

510
00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:12.190
Olivia, enjoying every second of this, just shrugs while looking at Zoe again.

511
00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:13.330
Oh, nothing.

512
00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.990
Just that some people don't respect boundaries.

513
00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.710
You know, like Anna.

514
00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:19.410
Oh, my gosh.

515
00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:22.490
And then, the plot twist that ends everything,

516
00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:25.150
Zoe turns to Damien and says,

517
00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:26.410
You told her?

518
00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:27.850
Boom.

519
00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.190
Nuclear explosion.

520
00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:30.990
Bethany stands up and leaves.

521
00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:31.970
Doesn't say a word.

522
00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:33.510
Zoe storms after her.

523
00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:36.110
Damien just sits there, fully deceased.

524
00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:38.590
Olivia sips her wine, pleased.

525
00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:44.210
And then, because Jake cannot let the moment sit in silence, he just sighs and goes,

526
00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:47.290
So, are we still talking about the book?

527
00:23:47.510 --> 00:23:48.110
Or?

528
00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:50.190
No, Jake.

529
00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:51.610
Clearly not.

530
00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:54.290
Anyway, book club is dead.

531
00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:55.970
Bethany broke up with Damien.

532
00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:58.030
Damien stopped being friends with Olivia.

533
00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:01.010
But at least Zoe and Damien are dating now.

534
00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:01.710
Oh.

535
00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.090
I was lucky enough to not catch any strays.

536
00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.950
But one thing's for sure, nobody's ever reading Anna Karenina again.

537
00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:11.270
Wow.

538
00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:12.350
Mm-hmm.

539
00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:13.370
Mm-hmm.

540
00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:15.770
That's a spicy book club story.

541
00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.820
Angela, have you ever been in a book club?

542
00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.180
And did it have drama?

543
00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:24.420
I mean, no.

544
00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:26.040
I've never been asked.

545
00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:27.100
Here's the thing.

546
00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:29.400
I have several friends in different book clubs.

547
00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:31.160
And I've never been invited.

548
00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:32.920
And it does not bump me at all.

549
00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:33.720
It really doesn't.

550
00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.900
I mean, I just have always thought, well, when the timing's right, it'll happen.

551
00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:41.880
And honestly, I don't have any free time right now with, like, all the kids and extracurricular

552
00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:42.360
activities.

553
00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:46.480
I'm like, I don't know when I'd be going to this book club or hosting a book club.

554
00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:55.540
But I do have a friend that is very miffed that she isn't in this one particular book

555
00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:55.840
club.

556
00:24:56.480 --> 00:25:01.760
And whenever we get together with this group of gals for other things, it's just me and

557
00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:03.260
her that's not in the book club.

558
00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:04.680
But I don't care.

559
00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:05.820
I don't care.

560
00:25:06.340 --> 00:25:11.680
And sometimes when we're all together, they will bring up a book they're reading or who

561
00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:16.560
hosted or how late their book club went that night or, oh, my gosh, that, you know, whatever,

562
00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:21.160
that gluten-free lemon tart you made was so good or what, some little tidbit from the

563
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:21.680
book club.

564
00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:25.000
And she literally is, like, seething.

565
00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:27.060
Well, because that's tacky.

566
00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:28.060
Well, I don't.

567
00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:29.340
I'm sorry to say.

568
00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:34.380
Who are these women who are, like, actively talking about their little club in front of

569
00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:36.100
two people who aren't in their club?

570
00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:37.280
Well, I don't care.

571
00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:41.320
It's like, this book club has been going for years before I was even in this.

572
00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:42.860
Like, circle of people.

573
00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:48.720
But anyway, what kind of tickles me is the other gal that's not in it.

574
00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:54.620
Now, just really, if anyone's paying attention, she does things like she goes, ugh, or she

575
00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:57.340
rolls for us when they talk about their club.

576
00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:58.020
Yes.

577
00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:00.800
Anyway, it just kind of cracks me up.

578
00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:02.160
And I'm just like, I don't know.

579
00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:06.660
Book clubs seem like a lot of, like, maintenance, like emotional maintenance.

580
00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:09.140
I've been in two book clubs.

581
00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.480
They both ended in disaster.

582
00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:12.980
Oh, my gosh!

583
00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:18.800
One of them, the first one I was in, I got invited to, and it was a very similar thing

584
00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:23.140
as your friend, which is that this was, like, a group of women that were all really good

585
00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:25.520
friends, and they, like, vacationed together.

586
00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:31.800
And there was, like, me and one other gal who were not invited on the vacations.

587
00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:35.800
And I didn't care, because honestly, I didn't want to vacation with them.

588
00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:38.840
But I didn't know them, you know?

589
00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:44.500
But, like, a good portion of the book club meeting would be taken up with making these

590
00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:44.960
plans.

591
00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:50.040
And I just sort of felt like, I just want to talk about the book.

592
00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:50.640
Right.

593
00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:52.940
I don't need to talk about everyone's vacation.

594
00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:56.660
But I get it because this is the time they're all together, and they can all look at their

595
00:26:56.660 --> 00:27:00.920
calendars, and they can all figure out where they want to go this summer or whatever for

596
00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:01.760
their girls' trip.

597
00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:04.520
I found it annoying for that reason.

598
00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:09.920
But then some drama happened from one of the vacations, and it ended up blowing up the

599
00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:10.400
whole book club.

600
00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:11.360
I don't even know what happened.

601
00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:12.760
I wasn't very good friends with them.

602
00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:18.180
This was, like, when I first moved to L.A., and I got sort of invited in, and I just needed

603
00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:18.980
something to do.

604
00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:21.760
I thought I was going to meet people, maybe make some friends.

605
00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:22.980
It did not work out.

606
00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:27.660
Well, the other thing I'm kind of learning by being the person not invited in the book

607
00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:35.140
club is that people are so—once they get their book club set, they are not taking any

608
00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:35.900
applications.

609
00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:36.580
No.

610
00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:38.160
Like, that's it.

611
00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:41.780
If you have a successful book club, you don't want to mess with the chemistry.

612
00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:43.720
That's why my feelings aren't hurt.

613
00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.740
Who knows what I'd bring into it, you know?

614
00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:48.560
Also, we got this one letter.

615
00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.700
Now, this isn't the one that I said, don't read, okay?

616
00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.140
But I was curious about this letter.

617
00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:57.340
Do you have to pay dues to be in some book clubs?

618
00:27:57.660 --> 00:27:58.680
Is that a thing?

619
00:27:58.820 --> 00:27:59.860
Listen to this letter, okay?

620
00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:00.300
Okay.

621
00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.920
It's from Jim in Manchester, United Kingdom.

622
00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:05.940
He writes and says,

623
00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:12.520
I was in a book club up until 1990 when I was deployed to the Gulf whilst in the Army.

624
00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.920
I wrote to say I had to leave giving reasons!

625
00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:17.120
Exclamation point.

626
00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:23.180
Part of our instructions for deploying on active service was not to take books, etc.

627
00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:24.740
Okay?

628
00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:25.320
Okay.

629
00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:32.600
However, the book club then sent me a bill for termination of membership, which eventually

630
00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:35.880
reached me in the desert and then continued to do so.

631
00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:42.240
I eventually wrote back extremely sarcastically explaining that I was on the cusp of war with

632
00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:43.740
several choice words thrown in.

633
00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:45.640
I have never heard from them since.

634
00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:48.120
I've never heard of this.

635
00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:50.740
Cassie, does your book club have dues?

636
00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:52.040
No.

637
00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:53.320
Isn't that interesting?

638
00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:55.920
I mean, just yours is the most sort of like very organized.

639
00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:58.060
It sounds like it's nationwide, probably.

640
00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:03.320
Yeah, we have people in like Florida, Georgia, and someone's mom just joined.

641
00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:06.540
So yeah, it's very casual in that sense.

642
00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.660
Okay, well, I just had never heard that.

643
00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:10.980
Sam, have you ever been in a book club?

644
00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:12.700
I'm in a book club right now.

645
00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:13.280
Wait, what?

646
00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:15.160
It just occurred to me.

647
00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:18.760
I don't know why during the weeks of planning, this didn't pop into my head.

648
00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:21.620
But I'm part of a book club called the Banned Book Club.

649
00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:25.520
We're reading all the books that were banned from schools this year.

650
00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.260
We just read Handmaid's Tale.

651
00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:28.100
It's by vote.

652
00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:29.220
We vote which one.

653
00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:32.480
And now we're halfway through Fahrenheit 451.

654
00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:33.080
Nice.

655
00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:34.060
Oh, wow.

656
00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:35.120
You can join if you want.

657
00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:39.280
Email bandbookclub25 at gmail.com.

658
00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:41.100
Did you like Margaret Atwood?

659
00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:41.640
I did.

660
00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:42.380
Yeah, it was great.

661
00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:43.040
She's great.

662
00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:43.340
Yeah.

663
00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:43.660
Yeah.

664
00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:46.460
And you don't have to pay dues for that book club.

665
00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:47.080
Okay.

666
00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:47.840
Completely free.

667
00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:48.720
Well, okay.

668
00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:49.500
Free book club.

669
00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:55.980
Banned Books is just making me think of the band Mallard that you thought.

670
00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:58.080
It was a banded Mallard.

671
00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.420
But you thought the duck had been banned?

672
00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:01.600
Yes, I did.

673
00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:02.900
I didn't know why.

674
00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:07.480
I did a whole deep dive in why this one Mallard wasn't allowed anymore.

675
00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:09.000
But also how I—

676
00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:10.160
It was a typo.

677
00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:14.300
I did not question it in any way.

678
00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:15.100
You're like, what did he do?

679
00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:16.520
I took you at your word.

680
00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:16.940
I know.

681
00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:22.940
That there was a duck that had managed to get banned from a whole state for bad behavior.

682
00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:23.520
Yeah.

683
00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:24.420
I know.

684
00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:25.380
We didn't question it.

685
00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:27.760
I was like, well, he must have done something wrong.

686
00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:29.860
All right.

687
00:30:30.020 --> 00:30:31.640
Well, let us know.

688
00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:33.620
Do you have to pay dues for your book club?

689
00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:35.220
I've never heard of that.

690
00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:37.700
Lady, why don't we take a break?

691
00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:39.860
Because you have a deep dive.

692
00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:40.840
I do.

693
00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:41.960
And it's so fun.

694
00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.420
On the origin of the afternoon tea.

695
00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:47.080
And I have another surprise I have to get ready.

696
00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:48.380
I've been so excited.

697
00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:49.380
We have surprises.

698
00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:50.180
I have a surprise.

699
00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:52.160
I have three bags I'm going to unpack.

700
00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:53.920
I have two bags I have to unpack.

701
00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:56.200
Well, you had two bags and some books.

702
00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:57.240
Well, let's get to it.

703
00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:57.520
All right.

704
00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:58.260
Let's do it.

705
00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:08.330
Okay, everybody.

706
00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:09.570
We are back.

707
00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:16.490
I am in studio with my eyes closed because Angela has insisted that I close my eyes because

708
00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:19.790
she's going to surprise me with something and I don't know what it is.

709
00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:20.410
Keep them closed.

710
00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:21.090
They're closed.

711
00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:21.990
I'm not looking.

712
00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:22.790
I'm not looking.

713
00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:24.570
Do not open your eyes yet.

714
00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:29.670
Oh, I'm hearing it sounds like ceramics.

715
00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:31.650
Okay, open your eyes.

716
00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:36.770
You guys all get an afternoon tea hat.

717
00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:39.650
Everyone gets a tea hat with roses from my garden.

718
00:31:39.850 --> 00:31:40.690
Jenna, you get black.

719
00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:42.650
Oh, thank you.

720
00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:43.730
Can you get this one?

721
00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:44.310
Thank you.

722
00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:46.010
Sam, you and I have the big bra rim.

723
00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:46.710
Thank you.

724
00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:47.630
Lady.

725
00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:50.810
These are flowers from my garden.

726
00:31:50.950 --> 00:31:52.010
And look at my teapot.

727
00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.410
Your teapot is a kitty cat.

728
00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:55.450
Oh, my God.

729
00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:57.530
I'll tell a whole story about it.

730
00:31:57.670 --> 00:31:57.770
Look.

731
00:31:58.630 --> 00:32:02.010
Lady, guess what I brought as my surprise?

732
00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:02.750
What?

733
00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:06.190
I brought tea fascinators for everyone.

734
00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:09.670
We have so much headwear today.

735
00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:11.850
This is amazing.

736
00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:13.850
We have to take a picture.

737
00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:16.810
We can do one with hats and one with fascinators.

738
00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:18.710
Thank you so much, Angela.

739
00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:20.590
And with roses from your garden.

740
00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:24.550
Yes, I picked the roses this morning and then I bought the hats and I put the

741
00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:26.790
roses in the little rim of the hat.

742
00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:28.770
You know, each hat has a band around it.

743
00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:30.290
So I tuck the roses in the band.

744
00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:34.290
Well, I love a tea party and I have fascinators.

745
00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:37.490
I brought in my four fascinators.

746
00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:38.470
What is a fascinator?

747
00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:39.790
It is.

748
00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:43.750
It's like what they wear to fancy weddings and things in England.

749
00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:44.230
Yes.

750
00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:45.670
And you can wear them to tea.

751
00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:53.670
One of these four fascinators is made by a famous, like very famous milliner, Evita Petty.

752
00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:57.630
Her hats are sold at the oldest hat shop in Uptown New York.

753
00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:58.130
Ooh.

754
00:32:58.130 --> 00:33:03.230
But you can also get them online at harlemsheaven.com.

755
00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:04.790
Well, lady.

756
00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:05.730
And I met her.

757
00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:06.770
You met her.

758
00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:07.630
Yes, ma'am.

759
00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:13.470
So I was thinking, actually, maybe before I do my deep dive, maybe we should share what

760
00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:14.770
you and I just did on break.

761
00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:16.550
Which is hilarious.

762
00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:20.870
Because we both surprised each other with tea.

763
00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:21.610
Yes.

764
00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:24.070
So I'm going to share about the origins of the tea party.

765
00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:28.510
But one of the things I brought was a little, it's like a tea set.

766
00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:29.910
It's a teacup with a plate.

